    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Indo-Greeks struck the first bilingual coins, Greek on one face and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> in Kharoshthi (at first Brahmi) on the other, with Indian animals and Buddhist symbols, a concession to another culture never before made in the Hellenic world.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a>, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a> from 165 to 130 BCE, carried the kingdom to its greatest extent, campaigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and is remembered in the Milinda Panha as a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> who became an arhat.</li><li>After Menander the realm splintered among some twenty known kings, lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermaeus">Hermaeus</a> (c. 80 BCE) and its centre to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a> in 48/47 BCE; Strato II and Strato III fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a> about 10 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Kings Euthydemus II, Pantaleon and Agathocles issued the world's first cupro-nickel coins around 180–170 BCE, an alloy then known only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-nickel was not used in coinage again until the 19th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

