9" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="273.9" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="273.9" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>c. 113 BCE: Heliodorus, ambassador of Antialcidas to the Shunga king Bhagabhadra, raises a pillar to Vāsudeva at Vidisha.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="520.2" x2="520.2" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="520.2" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="520.2" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>48/47 BCE: Hippostratus loses to the Indo-Scythian Azes I, who founds an Indo-Scythian dynasty in the central territories.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>c. 10 CE: The Northern Satrap Rajuvula conquers the eastern Punjab kingdom of Strato II and Strato III, the last Indo-Greek kings.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">180 BCE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">10 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Demetrius I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, son of Euthydemus, invades the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a> and perhaps Gandhara, founding the Indo-Greek realm.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 185–170 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Pantaleon and Agathocles strike the first bilingual coins, with Indian inscriptions in Brahmi, found as far east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 180/175 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Apollodotus I makes himself independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> as the first proper Indo-Greek king, ruling Gandhara and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">165–130 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a> rules the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a>, extends the kingdom to its greatest extent and campaigns across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 130 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Greco-Bactrian kingdom falls to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and Heliocles is probably killed; the Indo-Greeks are cut off from the Greek world.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 113 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Heliodorus, ambassador of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas">Antialcidas</a> to the Shunga king Bhagabhadra, raises a pillar to Vāsudeva at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">48/47 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Hippostratus loses to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a>, who founds an Indo-Scythian dynasty in the central territories.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 10 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a> conquers the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> kingdom of Strato II and Strato III, the last Indo-Greek kings.</span></li></ol>
