    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodotus-i.jpg" alt="Diodotus I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodotus_I_of_Bactria_wearing_the_diadem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> had lived in the northwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> relocated rebellious Greek communities there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest of 326 BCE left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> and cities such as Bucephala behind. In 305 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> ceded his eastern territories to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> for 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a marriage agreement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> seceded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a> around 250 BCE; Euthydemus, a Magnesian Greek, seized its throne about 230 BCE and withstood a three-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, who in about 206 BCE recognised him and offered a daughter to his son Demetrius.</p><p>Demetrius I is generally considered the Greco-Bactrian king who first launched the Greek expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, around 200 BCE, and so the founder of the Indo-Greek realm; the overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a> about 185 BCE is thought to have encouraged it. His coins have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley; he conquered those regions and perhaps Gandhara, though he struck no Indian coins. Bopearachchi believes he took the title "King of India", and he may have founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavana</a> era of 186/5 BCE. After his death the Bactrian kings Pantaleon and Agathocles struck bilingual coins found as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, and civil wars among them allowed Apollodotus I, from about 180/175 BCE, to rule Gandhara and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> as the first king independent of Bactria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bharuch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bharuch.jpg" alt="Bharuch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrSwamitemple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">સતિષચંદ્ર, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Very little is known of the Indo-Greek economy, though it appears to have been vibrant. The abundance of the coinage suggests large mining operations, particularly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, and an important monetary economy; the kings struck bilingual coins in both the Greek round standard and the Indian square standard, so money seems to have circulated through every part of society, and the Kunindas to the east and the Satavahanas to the south adopted Indo-Greek monetary conventions, which points to extensive cross-border trade. The coinage is counted among the most artistically brilliant of antiquity.</p><p>Trade ran in every direction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> about 128 BCE, found bamboo canes from Qiong and cloth from Shu in its markets and was told that merchants bought them in Shendu, northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a settled farming land like Bactria. Across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> the Ptolemies had built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myos-hormos">Myos Hormos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica">Berenike</a> for voyages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> delta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a>; Eudoxus of Cyzicus sailed to India about 130 BCE and returned with perfumes and gemstones, and by the time Indo-Greek rule was ending up to 120 ships a year left Myos Hormos for India. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> records old drachmas of Apollodotus and Menander still current at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavana</a> donors funded Buddhist caves at Karla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nasik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>, and Nasik's grapes were probably first brought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/menander-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menander-i.jpg" alt="Menander I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menander_I,_portrait_from_coinage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The coins show Hellenistic uniforms, helmets either round in the Greco-Bactrian style or the flat Macedonian kausia of Apollodotus I's coins, and weapons of spear, sword, longbow and arrows. Around 130 BCE the Central Asian recurve bow and its gorytos case appear on the coins of Zoilos I, a sign of contact and apparently alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, and by 90 BCE it was standard for Indo-Greek horsemen, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermaeus">Hermaeus</a>'s coins show. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Milinda</a> Panha describes Menander reviewing his fourfold army of elephants, cavalry, bowmen and foot soldiers.</p><p>The wars are known only in fragments. Justin records that a Demetrius, "king of the Indians", besieged the Bactrian Eucratides for four months with 60,000 men, only for Eucratides to break out with 300 and hold land as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> between about 170 and 150 BCE, until Menander reclaimed it. Patanjali, around 150 BCE, wrote of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a> besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saketa">Saketa</a> and Madhyamika, and the Yuga Purana prophesies that they would reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and cast down its mud walls before civil war called them home; whether such a campaign took place is disputed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a> has King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> driving a demoralised Yavana army back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. The Indo-Greeks were finally conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> ruled briefly in the northwest around 80 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a> defeated Hippostratus in 48/47 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a> took the last kingdom around 10 CE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antialcidas.jpg" alt="Antialcidas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas">Antialcidas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antialcidas_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Indo-Greek Kingdom was never one state but a number of Hellenistic kingdoms ruled from regional capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a>, Pushkalavati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagram">Bagram</a>. Thirty-six kings are known, most only from coins, and their sequence is still worked out from overstrikes and coin types, following Osmund Bopearachchi and R. C. Senior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a> and Apollodotus I were the first to put "Saviour King" on coins minted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a title that in the Greek world marked a great defensive victory, inscribed in Pali as "Tratarasa"; seven late kings around Gandhara, from Zoilos I to Peukolaos, called themselves Dharmikasa, "Follower of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>".</p><p>From Apollodotus I onward almost all coins were bilingual, Greek on the obverse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> in the Kharoshthi script on the reverse, and from Apollodotus II, about 80 BCE, Kharoshthi letters served as mintmarks beside Greek monograms, which suggests local technicians in the mints. These coins were the key by which James Prinsep and Carl Ludwig Grotefend deciphered Kharoshthi in 1835–36. The kings worshipped the Greek pantheon shown on their coins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, but also took part in Indian religions: Heliodorus, ambassador of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas">Antialcidas</a>, called himself a devotee of Vāsudeva on his pillar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> around 113 BCE, the first known inscription of the Bhagavata movement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a>, founded by Demetrius, mixed Greek and Indian elements without segregation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nagasena"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagasena.jpg" alt="Nagasena" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Milinda_ask_questions.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace van Ruith, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Indo-Greeks combined Greek and Indian languages and symbols on their coins and Greek and Indian ideas in their cities, and the diffusion of their culture is still felt, above all through Greco-Buddhist art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished under them, and Menander's rule was remembered as benevolent: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Milinda</a> Panha has him debating the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a>, converting and attaining arhatship, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says that when he died the cities under his rule contested the honour of his remains and divided his ashes among their monuments, as with the Buddha.</p><p>Craftsmen from the northwest, leaving mason's marks in Kharoshthi, carved the earliest reliefs at Sanchi around 115 BCE and the torana gateways of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> in 100–75 BCE, where a warrior in Greek dress with a Buddhist triratana on his sword guards the stupa; a Sanchi frieze of about 50–1 BCE shows devotees in Greek tunics playing the aulos, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavana</a> donors account for nearly half the dedications on the pillars of the Great Chaitya at Karla. The Yavana era of 186 BCE dated Gandharan Buddha statues for centuries: the Loriyan Tangai Buddha of "year 318" is about 143 CE. Yavanesvara translated the Yavanajataka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> work on horoscopy, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a>'s court; the Kunindas, Satavahanas and others copied Indo-Greek coinage, a legacy traceable to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> in the 4th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> who followed kept Greek mints, legends and gods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/azes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azes-i.jpg" alt="Azes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Azes_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploadalt, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Menander's death around 130 BCE there are no historical records of the Indo-Greeks at all, and their later history is reconstructed from coins and archaeology. In the same years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, pushed on by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, the last Greco-Bactrian king Heliocles was probably killed, and the Indo-Greeks were cut off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Graeco-Roman world</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the Yaudheya and Arjunayana republics struck coins celebrating victory, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> and Datta dynasties rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, where the Maghera inscription of the 116th year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a>, about 70 BCE, is the last trace of Greek rule. Philoxenus (100–95 BCE) briefly held everything from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paropamisadae">Paropamisadae</a> to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, but the territories fragmented again among some twenty kings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermaeus">Hermaeus</a>, one of the last important kings in the Paropamisadae, ruled until about 80 BCE; soon after his death the Yuezhi or Sakas took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and minted posthumous coins in his name until about 40 CE, when they merge into the coinage of the Kushan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a>. Around 80 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a>, perhaps a general in Indo-Greek service, ruled briefly in the northwest; Hippostratus (65–55 BCE) recovered ground until he lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes I</a>, who founded an Indo-Scythian dynasty in 48/47 BCE. Fleeing the Sakas, the Indo-Greeks kept a kingdom in the eastern Punjab until the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a> conquered Strato II and Strato III around 10 CE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Indo-Greek Kingdom comprised the Hellenistic Greek states of the northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, founded when Demetrius I crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> about 200 BCE and greatest under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a>, which joined Greek and Indian art, coinage and religion before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> ended the last of them around 10 CE.</em></p>

